        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 368,
        "headingId": 767,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:13:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128043,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 368,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Epithelioid haemangioendothelioma with <em>WWTR1</em>::<em>CAMTA1</em> fusion; epithelioid haemangioendothelioma with <em>YAP1</em>::<em>TFE3</em> fusion</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:13:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128044,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 368,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>EHE rarely involves the skin, with no site predilection {9855348; 9130982}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:13:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128045,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 368,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most are nondescript nodules, sometimes with a thickened epidermis {25782038; 9855348; 25386269} or ulceration {24354555}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:13:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128046,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 368,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>EHE most often arises in middle-aged adults, with a slight female predominance {25782038; 9130982; 9855348; 25386269}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:13:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128047,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 368,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:13:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128048,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 368,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>More than 90% of EHEs have t(1;3) with <em>WWTR1</em>::<em>CAMTA1</em> fusion {21584898; 21885404}; a rare subtype has a <em>YAP1</em>::<em>TFE3</em> fusion {23737213; 34381186}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:13:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128049,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 368,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:13:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128050,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 368,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>EHE is composed of relatively bland epithelioid cells, often with intracytoplasmic vacuoles, arranged in cords and nests, embedded in a variably myxohyaline stroma {7093931}. Vasoformative architecture is not seen in conventional EHE. The EHE subtype with <em>YAP1</em>::<em>TFE3</em> fusion has tumour cells with abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm, arranged in a solid or nested growth pattern, often forming vascular spaces {23737213; 34381186}.</p>\r\n<p>Immunohistochemically, EHE is positive for CD31, FLI1, ERG, and (less often) CD34. Low-molecular-weight cytokeratins are expressed in 20&ndash;30% of cases {11014572; 24986479; 34381186}. EHE with <em>WWTR1</em>::<em>CAMTA1</em> shows diffuse strong nuclear expression of CAMTA1 {26414223; 24986479}. Tumours with <em>YAP1</em>::<em>TFE3</em> fusion show diffuse nuclear TFE3 expression {23737213}; however, staining for TFE3 can also be seen in tumours with <em>WWTR1</em>::<em>CAMTA1</em> fusion {26414223; 24986479}. Loss of expression of the C-terminus of YAP1 is more specific for <em>YAP1</em>::<em>TFE3</em> fusions {34148063}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:13:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 775,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128051,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 368,
        "headingId": 775,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:13:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 833,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 776,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128052,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 368,
        "headingId": 776,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Identification of <em>WWTR1</em>::<em>CAMTA1</em> and <em>YAP1</em>::<em>TFE3</em> gene fusions is useful for subtyping.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:13:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 836,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 779,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128053,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 368,
        "headingId": 779,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> conventional EHE is composed of cords or nests of epithelioid cells with cytoplasmic vacuolization within a variably myxohyaline stroma; EHE with <em>YAP1</em>::<em>TFE3</em> fusion shows solid or nested growth, often with well-formed vascular channels, lined by epithelioid cells with abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable (in selected cases):</em> CAMTA1 expression by immunohistochemistry and/or <em>WWTR1</em>::<em>CAMTA1</em> fusion; TFE3 overexpression by immunohistochemistry and/or <em>TFE3</em> rearrangement (<em>YAP1</em>::<em>TFE3</em> fusion).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:13:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 834,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 777,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128054,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 368,
        "headingId": 777,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:13:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 835,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 778,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128055,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 368,
        "headingId": 778,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Approximately 20% of EHEs of soft tissues metastasize and pursue an aggressive clinical course {7093931; 9130982; 18551749; 24986479}. Cutaneous EHE has an excellent prognosis {9130982; 9855348}. The clinical behaviour of cutaneous EHE with <em>YAP1</em>::<em>TFE3</em> is unknown but may be less aggressive {34381186}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T12:13:21"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]